WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:02.320
Film Courage: Just to follow-up on what we
were talking about previously.

00:00:02.320 --> 00:00:05.300
There are two schools of thought, you have
people who say “You can’t be so precious

00:00:05.300 --> 00:00:06.340
about the work.

00:00:06.340 --> 00:00:08.630
You can’t hold it so tightly and be so paranoid”
(i.e., over-protective of your ideas/intellectual

00:00:08.630 --> 00:00:09.630
property).

00:00:09.630 --> 00:00:11.559
And then you have someone brilliant like Steve
Jobs who was incredibly secretive about ideas/upcoming

00:00:11.559 --> 00:00:12.559
products, etc.

00:00:12.559 --> 00:00:14.370
[From what we’ve read] you wouldn’t no
sometimes what was coming out from Apple or

00:00:14.370 --> 00:00:18.151
there were rumors in Silicon Valley, whatever
and we [The General Public] wouldn’t know

00:00:18.151 --> 00:00:20.540
until years later sometimes.

00:00:20.540 --> 00:00:23.480
Which this [secretive] style obviously worked
out for him in many ways.

00:00:23.480 --> 00:00:25.850
So it’s an interesting mix.

00:00:25.850 --> 00:00:29.880
How do we go in the middle where we are not
too paranoid about our work being taken (too

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:30.880
precious).

00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:39.550
I see some filmmakers who won’t even have
their trailer embeddable because they are

00:00:39.550 --> 00:00:45.260
afraid for whatever (maybe they just didn’t
click the button) but to that level versus

00:00:45.260 --> 00:00:49.149
someone that is an open book that is (unfortunately)
easily taken advantage of (and I’m not saying

00:00:49.149 --> 00:00:50.720
this happened in your case).

00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:53.030
Believing in the good in people (because you’re
good) but protecting yourself.

00:00:53.030 --> 00:00:55.470
I think there is a fine line.

00:00:55.470 --> 00:01:02.180
Gary W. Goldstein: Look there are two sides
to ever idea.

00:01:02.180 --> 00:01:07.820
And one is knowing the time and the place.

00:01:07.820 --> 00:01:16.180
A lot of filmmakers hold their cards so close
to their vest or their chest that it’s self-defeating.

00:01:16.180 --> 00:01:25.130
A classic example, most studio executives
in the marketing department will tell you

00:01:25.130 --> 00:01:28.670
that they are considered Satan by filmmakers.

00:01:28.670 --> 00:01:34.659
Filmmakers will not share one iota of their
film, one frame of their film, until it’s

00:01:34.659 --> 00:01:37.720
absolutely locked and loaded.

00:01:37.720 --> 00:01:41.500
Which means that the marketing department
has precious little time to think about it,

00:01:41.500 --> 00:01:44.590
be creative and come up with great concepts
and execute on them.

00:01:44.590 --> 00:01:49.990
It’s a last minute eleventh-hour deal and
it almost always is.

00:01:49.990 --> 00:01:53.070
Is that in the filmmaker’s best interest?

00:01:53.070 --> 00:01:54.390
I suspect not.

00:01:54.390 --> 00:02:02.860
I know that many, many years ago I did a picture
at Universal, with a young writer/director.

00:02:02.860 --> 00:02:04.119
His first directing gig.

00:02:04.119 --> 00:02:09.530
And together we went in with story boards
and I insisted that we have someone from every

00:02:09.530 --> 00:02:13.260
department present or two people from every
department (whatever could show up).

00:02:13.260 --> 00:02:21.650
We had 16 to 18 people in the room representing
every conceivable area of marketing.

00:02:21.650 --> 00:02:24.890
We presented to them and said “Look we just
came to say hi.

00:02:24.890 --> 00:02:30.500
We want to know what you do and we want you
to be aware of our little project because

00:02:30.500 --> 00:02:34.040
it’s not the biggest on your lot.

00:02:34.040 --> 00:02:37.430
We just want to share with you a little bit
about the genesis.

00:02:37.430 --> 00:02:38.620
We want to share some storyboards.

00:02:38.620 --> 00:02:40.879
By the way the storyboards suck!

00:02:40.879 --> 00:02:43.010
And you guys are so much smarter.

00:02:43.010 --> 00:02:46.650
It was just a way we could communicate to
you.

00:02:46.650 --> 00:02:54.280
But most importantly (before we leave and
shut up and have some Q&amp;A, what we really

00:02:54.280 --> 00:03:01.660
want from you is your guidance telling us
why we are on location shooting, what do we

00:03:01.660 --> 00:03:02.750
need to bring back?

00:03:02.750 --> 00:03:06.390
What do you need to do your job better.

00:03:06.390 --> 00:03:09.600
What kind of still images, what kind of b-roll?

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:14.770
What do we need to bring to you so you can
be the best?

00:03:14.770 --> 00:03:17.080
“ They literally said to us “We’re veterans.

00:03:17.080 --> 00:03:20.720
We’ve been here 15 years, 20 years, 25 years,
whatever!

00:03:20.720 --> 00:03:24.370
We’ve never, ever had filmmakers conduct
this kind of a meeting.”

00:03:24.370 --> 00:03:26.190
That was at Universal Pictures.

00:03:26.190 --> 00:03:27.200
Wow!

00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:28.209
Okay.

00:03:28.209 --> 00:03:38.830
So there is clearly this kind of tradition
in the human race to hold very, very dear,

00:03:38.830 --> 00:03:45.180
to keep very private what we consider our
IP [intellectual property], our baby.

00:03:45.180 --> 00:03:51.800
And I think sometimes it serves us and sometimes
it’s self-defeating.

00:03:51.800 --> 00:03:58.370
But I think you just have to be sober about
it and look at what other successful people

00:03:58.370 --> 00:04:02.670
do and how do they do it.

00:04:02.670 --> 00:04:08.879
The flip side of that is I never worry that
much about people stealing what I have.

00:04:08.879 --> 00:04:14.910
I have this crazy idea that each of us is
gifted and special in a way that we would

00:04:14.910 --> 00:04:20.540
do something that no one could steal any of
my ideas and do it the way I would do it.

00:04:20.540 --> 00:04:23.619
Let them do their version of it.

00:04:23.619 --> 00:04:29.180
So I can write a book or I can produce a movie,
I can nurture a recording artist or whatever

00:04:29.180 --> 00:04:32.659
those things are.

00:04:32.659 --> 00:04:36.319
An idea is worth very, very little in Hollywood.

00:04:36.319 --> 00:04:45.419
It’s the people and their ability to deliver
on an idea and birth out of nothing, that’s

00:04:45.419 --> 00:04:46.559
what we do!

00:04:46.559 --> 00:04:53.340
We birth out of thin air something very concrete,
very compelling (hopefully) that moves people

00:04:53.340 --> 00:04:57.259
(happily or sadly).

00:04:57.259 --> 00:05:00.550
I think it’s the doer (not the idea).

00:05:00.550 --> 00:05:06.270
So I think theft is actually an interesting
concept in a world of IP.

00:05:06.270 --> 00:05:09.349
And I don’t really take out an insurance
policy.

00:05:09.349 --> 00:05:10.439
I don't really worry about it.

00:05:10.439 --> 00:05:11.169
I don’t think about it.

